<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vijayalaya Chola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 848–<a href="/years/871">871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vijayalaya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> was the founder of the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-dynasty">Chola dynasty</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A local chief claiming descent from the early Cholas, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> from the Muttaraiyar chiefs around 848 and made it his capital, at first as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Pallavas. His successors built from this base the Chola Empire, which came to dominate southern India and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vijayalaya-chola.jpg" alt="Vijayalaya Chola" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Venu62 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vijayalaya_territories.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> kingdom, known from Tamil literature and from Greek merchants and geographers, faded after about 300 CE, a decline attributed to the invasion of the Kalabhras. The Cholas held on to their old capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraiyur">Uraiyur</a>, while a branch ruled in the Telugu lands as chieftains of the Pallavas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-cholas">Telugu Chodas</a>. Historians long could not trace Vijayalaya's ancestry. The Anbil and Velanjeri plates name a Sri Kantha before him, and the Velanjeri inscription calls his father Ottriyuran, read by some scholars as a title meaning lord of Thiruottriyur, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Around the middle of the 9th century the Pallavas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> were fighting for supremacy in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Vijayalaya used the opportunity to rise out of obscurity and take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> from Elango Mutharaiyar, the last ruler of the Mutharaiyar dynasty, with the help of a Muttaraiyar lieutenant, Sattan Paliyilli. He ruled the region north of the river Kaveri, and it is said that in 852 he fought and defeated the Pandyas.</p><p>His rise alarmed the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-iii">Nandivarman III</a>, who called on the Pandyan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varaguna-ii">Varagunavarman II</a>, then his subordinate ally, to suppress him. Varaguna led an expedition into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> country and reached the north bank of the Kaveri near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>. Vijayalaya, by then old and an invalid, lost his kingdom to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and became a tax-paying king under them, though the Cholas who came after him conquered the Pandya kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Tiruvalangadu plates record that Vijayalaya made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> his capital and built there a temple to the goddess Nisumbhasudani, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, and the Kanyakumari inscription says that he renovated the city. A Solesvara temple at Narttamalai in Pudukkottai is also attributed to him. He took the title Parakēcarivarmaṉ, great son who is a lion to his enemies, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> kings after him used the title Parakēcari. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya Chola I</a>, who laid the foundation of the imperial Chola state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vijayalaya Chola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The line revived in 848, when <b>Vijayalaya</b> took Thanjavur and began the Chola Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-cholas">Early Cholas</a></li></ul></section>
